.\" sockatmark.3 - Substrate libc
.TH SOCKATMARK 3 "2026-09-23" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
sockatmark \- determine whether a socket is at the out-of-band mark
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library
.RI ( libc ", " \-lc )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <sys/socket.h>
.PP
.BI "int sockatmark(int " sockfd ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR sockatmark ()
reports whether the next octet to be read from the socket
.I sockfd
is the one that follows TCP urgent data (the
.IR mark ).
.PP
When a TCP peer sends urgent data, the urgent octet is removed from the
stream and can be read with
.BR recv (2)
and
.BR MSG_OOB .
A normal read stops at the mark, so a reader that loops on
.BR read (2)
and checks
.BR sockatmark ()
can tell which data preceded the urgent notification.
The mark is cleared by the next read that returns data.
.PP
An AF_UNIX or datagram socket never has a mark.
.PP
The function is implemented with the
.B SIOCATMARK
.BR ioctl (2).
.SH RETURN VALUE
1 if the socket is at the mark, 0 if it is not.
On error, \-1 is returned, and
.I errno
is set to indicate the error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EBADF
.I sockfd
is not a valid file descriptor.
.TP
.B ENOTTY
.I sockfd
does not refer to a socket.
.SH CONFORMING TO
POSIX.1-2008.
.SH NOTES
Before Substrate 2026-09,
.BR sockatmark ()
returned 0 for every descriptor without consulting the kernel.
.SH EXAMPLE
Read up to the mark, then fetch the urgent octet:
.PP
.EX
char buf[512], oob;
for (;;) {
    int at = sockatmark(fd);
    if (at < 0) { perror("sockatmark"); break; }
    if (at) {
        if (recv(fd, &oob, 1, MSG_OOB) == 1)
            printf("urgent: %c\en", oob);
        break;
    }
    if (read(fd, buf, sizeof buf) <= 0)
        break;
}
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR recv (2),
.BR send (2),
.BR ioctl (2),
.BR fcntl (2),
.BR tcp (7)
